A||||4||batboy||steering wheel removal||||1
Z||000000||batboy||10-22-1999||09:15 PM||||I'm trying to take my steering wheel off, but it won't budge. I've removed the horn cover, the clip, and the large nut. I attached my steering wheel puller using the right size bolts (which I had to visit a bunch of hardware stores to find). But, the wheel is stuck on tight. I bent one of the bolts and I think it partially stripped the threads out on one of the steering wheel puller hole. Now what do I do? I've taken steering wheels off other cars before and never had any trouble. Help!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||10-23-1999||03:08 AM||||<BR>This probably isn't going to be of much help to you batboy, but mine came off real easy. However, that was over two years ago and I forget the finer details. This seems obvious, but can you soak 'er down with penetrating oil?<P>I guess I'm just trying to supply you with some moral support. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000002||batboy||10-23-1999||07:15 AM||||Thanks Pat, I need the moral support. Yes, I can try soaking it with penetrating oil, but don't know if that will help, nothing looks rusty in there. I will get a couple new bolts and try again, but I'm afraid I might have to tap one of those holes out to the next bigger size. Ogre said not to use a hammer on the steering column, but does anyone think I can tap on the puller and wheel a little next time as I tighten the puller to help loosen it up. Any suggestions at this point will be helpful.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||10-23-1999||09:23 AM||||Tighten The Puller Up Real Tight And Give It A Tap,. Dont Hit It Like You Are Trying To Break It But Give It A Nice Blow I Dont Think You Will Hurt It. I Have Gotten Many Off Without A Puller Using The Hammer. But The Puller Is Safer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-23-1999||12:33 PM||||if it's not a tilt column hitting the shaft wont hurt it much. In a tilt unit you risk damage to the tilt joint if you hit it very hard. the joint is plastic.<P>get a better puller kit. sounds like the one you got is shot. Always oil the main puller bolt before you start. I've never seen a wheel stay on with a good puller kit. I usually use a 1/2 inch drive ratchet my puller, but a cheap puller will fold up on you if you do that.||||reg||1
